1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:00,929
Hey everyone.

2
00:00:01,487 --> 00:00:04,685
I'm Brindal, a senior
software engineer at Coinbase.

3
00:00:05,128 --> 00:00:06,062
Thanks for joining me.

4
00:00:06,694 --> 00:00:08,383
A quick note before we start.

5
00:00:08,967 --> 00:00:13,649
This talk is based on my own
experience and independent work.

6
00:00:13,843 --> 00:00:18,327
It's not affiliated with
or endorsed by any company.

7
00:00:19,401 --> 00:00:23,532
Today, we are talking about
building an internal developer

8
00:00:23,532 --> 00:00:25,480
platform for generative AI.

9
00:00:26,002 --> 00:00:28,993
We'll explore how to move
from raw foundation models to

10
00:00:29,005 --> 00:00:31,345
governed self-service APIs.

11
00:00:32,234 --> 00:00:37,716
First, let's look at what happens when
teams adopt AI without a platform.

12
00:00:39,713 --> 00:00:44,183
Imagine this scenario: A background
service runs into an unhandled

13
00:00:44,270 --> 00:00:46,901
timeout and enters a fast retry loop.

14
00:00:47,499 --> 00:00:51,214
Every single retry hits an
expensive foundation model.

15
00:00:51,844 --> 00:00:58,575
And because teams were moving fast, that
service was using shared API key directly

16
00:00:58,579 --> 00:01:00,546
pasted in their configuration file.

17
00:01:01,557 --> 00:01:06,146
By the next morning, thousands
of calls had fired, and the

18
00:01:06,146 --> 00:01:07,725
cloud bill was enormous.

19
00:01:08,724 --> 00:01:13,211
When the on-call engineer woke
up, they faced two problems.

20
00:01:14,533 --> 00:01:18,423
First, they couldn't tell which
service caused the surge, and second,

21
00:01:18,469 --> 00:01:22,662
revoking the shared key would break
the production for every other team.

22
00:01:23,728 --> 00:01:27,783
When you look closely, that
wasn't an AI model problem.

23
00:01:28,379 --> 00:01:32,389
It was a missing platform
contract And here's what that

24
00:01:32,390 --> 00:01:34,598
contract looks like in practice.

25
00:01:35,315 --> 00:01:40,291
The fix is an internal developer
platform, or in short, IDP.

26
00:01:41,092 --> 00:01:42,733
Think of it as a paved road.

27
00:01:43,353 --> 00:01:48,030
Sometimes I believe the easy
way is also the safe way.

28
00:01:48,888 --> 00:01:53,436
So basically, a product team asks
for access with one small YAML

29
00:01:53,445 --> 00:01:55,668
file in a single pull request.

30
00:01:56,695 --> 00:01:58,700
In return, they get three things.

31
00:01:59,210 --> 00:02:04,973
First, a model alias, no vendor SDK,
and no key pasted into their repo.

32
00:02:05,559 --> 00:02:08,390
Second, a budget with an assigned owner.

33
00:02:08,913 --> 00:02:15,110
And third, a distributed trace for every
single call Now, why do we need this?

34
00:02:16,290 --> 00:02:20,300
Today, every team wires its
own client to every provider.

35
00:02:20,850 --> 00:02:24,311
That creates an N times
M integration mess.

36
00:02:25,210 --> 00:02:30,801
We end up with keys scattered across
repos, no clear owner for spend,

37
00:02:31,267 --> 00:02:37,028
uncoordinated rate limits, and the exact
same prompt paid for again and again.

38
00:02:38,020 --> 00:02:41,026
With a platform, there's
one single front door.

39
00:02:41,732 --> 00:02:46,103
You get one key per team, one
budget, one trace, and one

40
00:02:46,119 --> 00:02:47,994
clean place to switch models.

41
00:02:49,163 --> 00:02:51,479
Let's follow one live request through it

42
00:02:52,479 --> 00:02:56,777
So in this design, a request
passes through six stages.

43
00:02:57,415 --> 00:03:01,003
First, a gateway checks who's calling.

44
00:03:01,563 --> 00:03:06,593
The connection uses mutual
TLS or mTLS, and the token

45
00:03:06,593 --> 00:03:08,653
says which tenant is calling.

46
00:03:09,163 --> 00:03:11,813
Here, a tenant is simply the calling team.

47
00:03:12,744 --> 00:03:15,074
Next, it cleans the request.

48
00:03:15,683 --> 00:03:21,429
So personal data or PII and secrets
are removed before anything leaves.

49
00:03:22,284 --> 00:03:26,504
An Envoy filter called external
processing does this work.

50
00:03:27,514 --> 00:03:28,997
Then comes the decision.

51
00:03:29,580 --> 00:03:34,223
Open Policy Agent, or OPA in
short, checks the team's rules.

52
00:03:35,256 --> 00:03:39,366
If the call is allowed, the gateway
places a hold on the team's budget.

53
00:03:40,546 --> 00:03:44,719
Now, the gateway checks the cache,
which is scoped to each tenant.

54
00:03:45,216 --> 00:03:48,730
If it finds a safe match,
it answers right away.

55
00:03:49,275 --> 00:03:51,973
It releases the hold and
no tokens are charged.

56
00:03:52,757 --> 00:03:56,523
If there is no match, the
gateway routes the call to model.

57
00:03:58,086 --> 00:04:02,513
And when the answer comes back, it
settles the bill, it charges the real

58
00:04:02,515 --> 00:04:04,938
token, and releases the rest of the hold.

59
00:04:06,633 --> 00:04:12,164
Every one of these stages emits
a metrics or OpenTelemetry span,

60
00:04:12,575 --> 00:04:14,476
so you can see the whole journey.

61
00:04:15,239 --> 00:04:17,417
And this isn't the only for chat.

62
00:04:18,126 --> 00:04:21,490
Agent tool calls like
MCP uses the same door.

63
00:04:22,280 --> 00:04:25,957
The same policy checks the
tool name and its arguments.

64
00:04:26,913 --> 00:04:32,449
From here, we'll focus on three main
ideas: budget holds, safe caching, and

65
00:04:32,481 --> 00:04:36,989
tested failover Let's go over idea one.

66
00:04:37,775 --> 00:04:41,941
So tokens are money, so budget
them like a card payment.

67
00:04:43,047 --> 00:04:46,736
But here's the problem: you
don't know the output cost until

68
00:04:46,736 --> 00:04:48,744
the model finishes answering.

69
00:04:49,640 --> 00:04:54,926
Ten parallel requests can all pass an
under-budget check at once, causing

70
00:04:54,928 --> 00:04:56,963
a messing-- massive out-- overrun.

71
00:04:58,223 --> 00:04:59,938
Step one, authorize.

72
00:05:00,710 --> 00:05:04,916
So the gateway places a hold for
the worst case, the input tokens

73
00:05:04,947 --> 00:05:06,986
plus the maximum output tokens.

74
00:05:07,549 --> 00:05:08,857
Step two, capture.

75
00:05:09,154 --> 00:05:12,717
It charges only what the
response actually reports.

76
00:05:13,470 --> 00:05:18,292
For streaming, it uses the provider's
final usage report when it sends one.

77
00:05:19,156 --> 00:05:25,268
If the stream breaks, it tries to
cancel upstream, settles what was used,

78
00:05:25,286 --> 00:05:27,057
and releases the rest of the hold.

79
00:05:27,930 --> 00:05:29,073
Step three, release.

80
00:05:29,333 --> 00:05:32,876
The rest goes back, straight
back to the team's budget.

81
00:05:34,426 --> 00:05:39,260
The check and the hold happens in
one atomic step, so parallel requests

82
00:05:39,266 --> 00:05:42,473
can't spend the same budget twice.

83
00:05:44,312 --> 00:05:46,345
Now let's look at idea two.

84
00:05:48,039 --> 00:05:51,668
Similar is not the same
when money is involved.

85
00:05:52,412 --> 00:05:55,529
A semantic cache matches
prompts by meaning.

86
00:05:56,185 --> 00:06:01,556
For example, "Send one hundred dollars
to my savings," and, "Send one thousand

87
00:06:01,557 --> 00:06:07,793
dollar-- dollars to my savings," look
almost identical to a vector model, but

88
00:06:07,793 --> 00:06:10,161
the real-world answers are very different.

89
00:06:10,870 --> 00:06:15,532
Also, after redaction, an account
balance question looks like the

90
00:06:15,560 --> 00:06:17,560
exact same text for every user.

91
00:06:19,267 --> 00:06:23,837
So before serving a cached answer,
the gateway asks four questions.

92
00:06:24,367 --> 00:06:26,571
First, is it in the same scope?

93
00:06:27,320 --> 00:06:32,317
The tenant, model, system prompt,
and decoding settings must all match.

94
00:06:33,132 --> 00:06:35,239
Second, is it close enough?

95
00:06:35,798 --> 00:06:41,286
The similarity has to pass a
threshold you tune for your model.

96
00:06:42,243 --> 00:06:44,681
Third, are the entities the same?

97
00:06:45,161 --> 00:06:49,912
The gateway pulls out amounts,
accounts, dates, and, compare--

98
00:06:50,320 --> 00:06:52,451
compares their hashes.

99
00:06:53,070 --> 00:06:57,594
So it can check for a match without
storing a raw account number.

100
00:06:58,390 --> 00:07:01,744
And fourth, should this
prompt be cached at all?

101
00:07:02,436 --> 00:07:06,033
Transactional and per user
prompts never uses the cache.

102
00:07:06,898 --> 00:07:12,051
If all four answers are yes,
serve the cached answer.

103
00:07:12,554 --> 00:07:18,165
If any answer is no, call the model
You don't need all of this on day one.

104
00:07:18,280 --> 00:07:23,112
Caching is ladder, climbing only as high
as your checks can follow, basically.

105
00:07:24,104 --> 00:07:26,078
Start with the provider's prompt cache.

106
00:07:26,390 --> 00:07:29,946
It reuses shared prompt
prefixes, not previous answers.

107
00:07:30,423 --> 00:07:34,906
Next, add an exact match cache,
same request, same answer.

108
00:07:35,313 --> 00:07:40,205
Then move up the scope, semantic
cache, protected behind our four

109
00:07:40,207 --> 00:07:41,937
checks, we discussed earlier.

110
00:07:42,764 --> 00:07:48,380
And at the top, for self-hosted models,
there is a KV cache-aware routing.

111
00:07:48,829 --> 00:07:50,254
KV means key value here.

112
00:07:51,002 --> 00:07:55,420
The gateway sends each request to
the GPU worker, where prefixes,

113
00:07:56,026 --> 00:08:01,844
are already warm in memory Now,
what if a model provider fails?

114
00:08:03,022 --> 00:08:05,549
Then the idea three comes in the picture.

115
00:08:06,044 --> 00:08:08,722
Failover only to models you have tested.

116
00:08:09,722 --> 00:08:13,690
An untested backup might not
follow your JSON schema, or

117
00:08:13,719 --> 00:08:16,330
it- its answers might be worse.

118
00:08:17,029 --> 00:08:20,954
Evaluation runs in CI
on every model change.

119
00:08:21,275 --> 00:08:24,353
The runtime router never
runs slow evals live.

120
00:08:24,755 --> 00:08:27,438
It only checks an in-memory passed flag.

121
00:08:28,125 --> 00:08:33,647
Say the primary model fails with a
five hundred errors, a timeout or

122
00:08:33,647 --> 00:08:38,675
four twenty-nine rate limiting error,
the router takes the next candidate

123
00:08:38,676 --> 00:08:40,283
from the tenant's approved list.

124
00:08:41,002 --> 00:08:42,882
Did it pass this tenant's evals?

125
00:08:43,428 --> 00:08:47,715
If yes, route to it and tag
the trace spans as a failover.

126
00:08:48,142 --> 00:08:50,400
If no, skip it and try the next one.

127
00:08:51,382 --> 00:08:54,694
If none are left, return
a clear retrieval error.

128
00:08:55,407 --> 00:08:57,692
Never allow a silent degr- downgrade.

129
00:08:58,692 --> 00:09:00,598
Here's the decide stage as code.

130
00:09:01,404 --> 00:09:04,748
Rego for OPA one point X.

131
00:09:05,450 --> 00:09:06,745
The default is denied.

132
00:09:07,362 --> 00:09:11,307
A call is allowed only if the model
is on the tenant's lease, tenant's

133
00:09:11,338 --> 00:09:16,566
leased, the data scan is clean, and
the hold fits the available budget.

134
00:09:17,072 --> 00:09:22,886
If the hold doesn't fit, some tenants
can degrade to a smaller internal model.

135
00:09:23,794 --> 00:09:26,328
It produces one decision object.

136
00:09:26,768 --> 00:09:32,232
Allow and degrade can never both match,
and everything else falls back to deny.

137
00:09:33,211 --> 00:09:37,934
The redaction scan itself runs
in a DLP or data loss prevention.

138
00:09:38,559 --> 00:09:44,798
Filter inside Envoy execution proc-
process, OPA only reads its findings.

139
00:09:45,798 --> 00:09:48,987
All of this leaves one trace per request.

140
00:09:49,451 --> 00:09:51,844
It tells you who spent what and why.

141
00:09:52,390 --> 00:09:53,889
Three practical tips here.

142
00:09:54,367 --> 00:09:59,017
First, name model spans the
OpenTelemetry way, combining

143
00:09:59,018 --> 00:10:00,710
the operation and model alias.

144
00:10:01,408 --> 00:10:05,940
Second, keep infrastructure details
like the gateway's Kubernetes

145
00:10:05,957 --> 00:10:07,723
namespace on the resource.

146
00:10:08,956 --> 00:10:11,497
Put the calling tenant
on each request span.

147
00:10:11,986 --> 00:10:14,641
That's how you charge usage
back to the right team.

148
00:10:15,506 --> 00:10:18,624
And third, pin your schema version.

149
00:10:19,371 --> 00:10:23,466
The OpenTelemetry Gen AI
conventions are still in

150
00:10:23,466 --> 00:10:25,169
development status and can change.

151
00:10:26,815 --> 00:10:28,071
Where should you start?

152
00:10:28,547 --> 00:10:29,336
Here's my take.

153
00:10:29,690 --> 00:10:33,866
Begin with the quick wins, one
gateway with per team keys,

154
00:10:34,307 --> 00:10:38,401
provider prompt caching, and
token attribution in your traces.

155
00:10:39,378 --> 00:10:41,188
Then plan for the big bets.

156
00:10:41,696 --> 00:10:43,491
Those are today's three core ideas.

157
00:10:44,073 --> 00:10:49,315
And early on, avoid writing a gateway
from scratch, and don't spend time

158
00:10:49,319 --> 00:10:51,971
training your own embedding models

159
00:10:52,971 --> 00:10:57,248
Here are four common mistakes to
catch during architecture reviews.

160
00:10:57,806 --> 00:11:01,771
First, don't let every
tenant share one cache.

161
00:11:02,157 --> 00:11:03,169
Scope the keys.

162
00:11:03,770 --> 00:11:07,085
Second, don't check
budgets after the call.

163
00:11:07,718 --> 00:11:09,035
Place the hold before it.

164
00:11:09,758 --> 00:11:13,517
Third, don't failover to
any model that answers.

165
00:11:13,787 --> 00:11:16,267
Use only model that passed your checks.

166
00:11:16,918 --> 00:11:20,606
And fourth, don't embed
prompts before redactions.

167
00:11:21,575 --> 00:11:29,640
Redact first and treat redacted prompts as
per user or don't cache the, them at all.

168
00:11:30,578 --> 00:11:35,230
Remember, a fast wrong answer
is worse than a clear error.

169
00:11:36,230 --> 00:11:41,216
To wrap up, treat models like
infrastructure and tokens like money.

170
00:11:42,347 --> 00:11:44,579
Please remember these three principles.

171
00:11:45,614 --> 00:11:48,383
One, govern tokens like money.

172
00:11:48,739 --> 00:11:50,385
Hold, capture, release.

173
00:11:50,982 --> 00:11:56,708
Two, cache only what passes all
four checks: scope, similarity,

174
00:11:56,734 --> 00:11:59,117
entities, and intent every time.

175
00:11:59,915 --> 00:12:04,610
Three, failover only to
models that passed your evals.

176
00:12:06,289 --> 00:12:10,092
Now, if you want to go deeper,
scan the QR code on the screen.

177
00:12:10,564 --> 00:12:14,794
You'll find my articles on AI
and distributed system and the

178
00:12:14,794 --> 00:12:16,271
notes for this talk as well.

179
00:12:17,105 --> 00:12:18,174
Thanks for watching.
